WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.750
Apple’s 2019 MacBook Pro has now been in
our office for over a week, and we’ve discovered

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.340
some pretty amazing changes in our in-depth
testing, but before I get into that, let me

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.960
quickly remind you of what all went down with
last year’s MacBook Pro release.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.840
When the 2018 MacBook Pro with the new 6-core
i9 processor hit the hands of reviewers, some

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.270
major thermal management problems quickly
arose, and although Apple quickly fixed patched

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:27.869
some of those issues, it was too late, the
i9 MacBook Pro was already known for thermal

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:29.360
throttling in the mind of the consumer.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.519
And it’s true, we tested it ourselves and
found almost no difference between either

00:00:33.519 --> 00:00:39.210
of the three six-core CPU options, so we told
people to just go with the base six-core processor.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:44.260
On top of that, 2018 MacBook Pros started
experiencing the same major keyboard issues

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.700
as before, even after Apple’s attempts to
fix it with a silicon barrier beneath the

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:49.700
keys.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:54.049
Fortunately, some positive news came when
Apple updated the MacBook Pro with new Vega

00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:58.839
graphics, and after testing the Vega 20 option,
we noticed better thermals for the whole system

00:00:58.839 --> 00:01:03.609
overall and much, much better graphics performance
in video editing and gaming.

00:01:03.609 --> 00:01:08.530
Now when Apple announced that the 2019 MacBook
Pro will come with an 8-core processor, a

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:13.310
lot of people laughably assumed that the throttling
issues would get even worse, but after some

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:17.990
testing, it became quickly apparent that those
assumptions were wrong, WAY wrong, but before

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.930
we get into that, let’s talk about what’s
changed since last year.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:25.920
Well, not much, literally just the processor
options and the keyboard have changed.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.670
Everything else including the design, display,
trackpad, speakers, battery and the rest of

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:33.439
the internals are basically the same.

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:35.479
But don’t take that as a bad thing.

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:40.109
Yes, it’s been 4 years using this same design,
and everyone’s eagerly waiting for a complete

00:01:40.109 --> 00:01:44.140
redesign of the MacBook Pro, but in reality,
it’s already great.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.920
It’s thin, sleek, and machined out of a
solid block of aluminum.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.810
The speaker quality tops any laptop on the
market and the same thing goes for the very

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.979
reliable force-touch trackpad.

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:58.009
The battery life is excellent and the display
quality is great, with top-of-the-line color

00:01:58.009 --> 00:02:00.890
accuracy with its P3-Wide Color gamut support.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:05.549
The only two things I would personally change
are thinner bezels on the display and of course,

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:10.030
the butterfly keyboard, which lacks a good
level of key travel and more importantly,

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:15.050
has been crippled with reliability issues
since 2016, causing a LOT of consumers to

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.210
think twice about buying a MacBook Pro.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:18.840
Now here’s where it gets interesting.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.860
According to iFixit’s teardown of the new
2019 MacBook Pro, Apple has changed the material

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:29.500
of the switch covers from a tacky silicon-like
material to smooth nylon, along with updating

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.690
the metal dome switches as well.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.710
This could mean that Apple has figured out
what’s been causing the keyboard issues

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.880
and finally fixed it for good.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.570
Only time will tell, but if it turns out to
be true, this is a great time to buy a new

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:41.580
MacBook Pro.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:46.070
Among the list of possible causes for keyboard
failure, prolonged exposure to high levels

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.870
of heat from the processor is one of them,
so let’s get into thermal throttling.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:55.290
Here’s our configuration of the 2019 MacBook
Pro as well as the 2018 model we compared

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.370
it to, both priced at $3,549.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:02.790
When we ran our 5-run Cinebench R20 thermal
throttling test, we were surprised to see

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.770
that the 8-core hit a higher peak turbo boost
speed and ran at almost the same stabilized

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:12.970
clock speed after being at full load for 10
minutes, despite having a base clock speed

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:16.010
rated 300MHz lower than the 2018 model.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:20.700
Not only that, but it did it at a lower stabilized
temperature, which could possibly help the

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.830
reliability of the keyboard as well.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:28.400
On top of that, it managed to score 30% higher
in this benchmark, all while running cooler.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.670
And it doesn’t end there.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:34.510
When Max ran his video editing benchmark suite,
he noticed that the new 2019 MacBook Pro’s

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.340
fans were much quieter than the 2018 model.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:42.760
As you can see here, in the same exact test,
the 2018 MacBook Pro’s fans are at full

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.160
blast, 100% maxed out, while the 2019 model
is literally sitting just above idle, and

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.040
it finished with a faster score.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.060
One thing we also noticed was that the 2019
model operated a much lower temp at idle,

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:58.160
35 degrees Celsius instead of 45, now that’s
a pretty big difference.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.160
So what gives?

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.580
How is all of this even possible?

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.400
Well, there’s a couple of reasons.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.590
First off, Snazzy Labs discovered that Apple
has greatly improved the thermal paste on

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:10.620
the 2019 MacBook Pro, which is why we saw
lower temps at idle.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.540
Also, the new 8-core is using Intel’s 9th
gen technology, and it seems like Intel finally

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.120
got the thermals down to where the MacBook
Pro can properly manage them.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.460
Of course, the same chip in a beefier laptop
or PC with watercooling will run faster and

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.780
cooler, but if you consider that this chip
is in a super-thin MacBook Pro chassis, you

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:30.780
really start to appreciate the performance.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.620
In fact, Linus Tech Tips tested it in Windows
10 using Bootcamp and found that it thermal

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.240
throttled like crazy and performed much worse
compared to using macOS.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.090
It actually outperformed the same chip in
a much larger Windows Laptop in some tests,

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:49.180
so that means Apple is doing some sophisticated
undervolting to achieve this level of performance

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:50.650
in such a thin chassis.

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:52.130
So how is the performance?

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:55.850
Well, since there’s no difference at all
in graphics between these identically-priced

00:04:55.850 --> 00:05:02.220
Vega 20-packing MacBook Pros, we don’t see
much of a difference in standard 4K or H.265

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.410
codec editing, since those rely mainly on
graphics and hardware encoding.

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:11.590
In fact, the CPU utilization was lower on
the 2019 model in some of these tests, which

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:15.270
means there was extra processor performance
available that the MacBook Pro didn’t need

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:19.410
to use because the Vega 20 graphics was already
running at full blast.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:24.210
However, if we take a look at video editing
that’s heavily dependant on processor performance,

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:30.030
like this 4.5K R3D RAW export, we start to
see around 22% faster export speeds in Final

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.030
Cut Pro.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:35.050
So for graphics-heavy workloads, the Vega
20 graphics isn’t allowing the 8-core CPU

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:39.090
to run at its full potential, so for those
whose work is heavily dependant on graphics

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:43.630
performance, only buy the 8-core MacBook Pro
if you’re also gonna upgrade to Vega 20

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:44.630
graphics.

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:49.370
We’d also recommend not upgrading to the
2.4GHz 8-core processor because if you compare

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:54.810
benchmark results in Cinebench R20, you only
get around 2% faster performance for an extra

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:57.280
$200, which is not worth it at all.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.780
If you’re confused about which configuration
to buy, definitely check out our 2019 MacBook

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:02.780
Pro buyer’s guide.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:07.490
Now if we switch over to processor-heavy tasks
like photo editing using Lightroom classic,

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:09.950
the 8-core processor starts to show off a
bit.

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:15.980
Importing 40 42MP raw images from the A7R3
only took 5 seconds with each laptop.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:20.360
The editing experience is very smooth with
both laptops, the only time I could tell a

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:24.550
difference is with large brushes that have
a variety of stacked changes.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:29.860
The new Macbook is perfectly smooth while
the 2018 suffers from a little bit of stutter,

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:32.450
but keep in mind these are huge raw files.

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:37.460
We created smart previews to get rid of any
slowdowns and the 2019 was just 4 seconds

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:38.610
faster (23 seconds vs 27)

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:43.419
Exporting all 50 42MP Edited images to JPEG
was about 20% faster with the 2019 mainly

00:06:43.419 --> 00:06:44.470
because of the extra cores.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:45.470
(2 min 40 sec vs 3 min 17 sec)

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:49.120
So you do get a small performance bump in
processor-heavy workloads, but if we focus

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.800
on graphics, there isn’t much of a performance
boost this year looking at top-spec models

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.480
since there isn’t a new graphics chip alongside
the new 8-core processor.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.740
But what really impressed us was the improvements
to thermal management, and if Apple did, in

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:05.650
fact fix the keyboard issues for good, we
think this might be the best year to buy a

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:07.240
MacBook Pro in years.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:11.890
Now if you’re not looking to spend around
$3500 for a top-spec model, we think the base

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:16.340
$2400 15” MacBook Pro is going to offer
excellent value for the money.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:21.650
And we fully expect the base 6-core processor
to outperform the 6-core i9 from last year

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:23.720
thanks to better thermal management, and considering
that was a $400 upgrade, the base $2400 2019

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:24.720
model offers the best value yet.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:25.720
But wait, don’t order just yet.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:26.720
There have been rumors of Apple working on
a brand new redesigned 16” MacBook Pro,

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.720
which might come next year, so if you tend
to hold onto your devices for a few years,

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:28.720
you should probably wait.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:29.720
Overall, the 2019 MacBook Pro is an exceptional
laptop in terms of everything from design,

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:33.590
speaker quality and battery life, to things
like the display, trackpad and performance.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:39.370
Now that it’s 2019, donglegate is just about
over with tons of USB-C cable, adapter and

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:44.520
accessory options flooding the marketplace,
like this simple, cheap and compact USB-C

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.560
to USB-A adapter on Amazon, which we’ll
leave a link to down in the description.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.230
That, and if we consider the fact that high-end
Windows laptops have been getting more and

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:54.590
more expensive in recent years, we think there’s
never been a better time to buy a MacBook

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:55.590
Pro.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:58.460
If you enjoyed this review, give us a quick
like and tap the circle above to subscribe.

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:05.360
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next video!

